# Observed: 3182 edits across 61 files apply cleanly with 0 conflicts, but the
# rebuild then fails with 179 stale-name errors in 11 files.
#
# History, so the trend is legible: 18081 errors in 52 files originally; 2993 in
# 36 once colliding renames were skipped; 739 in 16 once macro arguments were
# renamed and unrewritable macro references started vetoing the rename; 621 in
# 14 once fields of a class nested in a class template mapped back to their
# pattern; 490 once partial and explicit specializations stopped being matched
# against the primary template; 179 once qualified dependent names resolved.
#
# Everything above was a *missed* rename -- the reference kept the old name and
# the build broke, loudly. What is left is the opposite and more dangerous kind:
# the rename is applied and the new name means something else. See below.
#
# NB: this file is sourced by bash, so values must not contain backticks or $.
REPO=googletest
RULESET=member_snake_case
EXPECT=known_fail
EXPECT_FAIL_PHASE=rebuild
EXPECT_FAIL_REASON='Unsafe new names. collides() only asks whether the new name is taken in the declarations own DeclContext, so three ways of landing on a name that means something else all get through. (1) A keyword: char_ becomes char, so gtest_unittest.cc gets "char char;" -- 23 errors. (2) An active macro: errno_ becomes errno, and glibc defines errno, so the ctor-init becomes an old-style base initializer -- most of gmock-actions.h, 82 errors. (3) Shadowed at the use site, not the declaration site: OnCallSpec::action_ becomes action inside WillByDefault(const Action<F>& action), so "action_ = action" becomes the self-assignment "action = action" -- gmock-spec-builders.h, 28 errors. (3) is the one that can compile and silently mean something else; these only fail loudly here by luck.'
